* {{alter|de|Haufe||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From a merger of {{inh|de|gmh|hūfe}} ({{m|gmh|hūf}}), {{m|gmh|houf}} ({{m|gmh|houfe}}), from {{inh|de|goh|hūfo}} (more southern) and {{m|goh|houf}} (more northern), from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*hūpô}} and its [[ablaut]] variant {{m|gem-pro|*haupaz}}, respectively. With the former is cognate {{cog|gml|hūpe}}, with the latter {{cog|nl|hoop}}, {{cog|en|heap}}.

Already in Middle High German the two forms began to influence each other such that the originally weak {{m|gmh||hūfe}} developed the strong byform {{m|gmh||hūf}}, while the originally strong {{m|gmh||houf}} developed the weak byform {{m|gmh||houfe}}. In Modern Standard German the vowels ''-ū-'' and ''-ou-'' regularly coalesced into ''-au-'', which completed the merger. The strong stem is preserved in {{m|de|zuhauf}}.

